---
import Workshop from '../../layouts/Workshop.astro';

const prepare = [
  {
    term: 'On the bench',
    text: 'A repair order with a diagnosed fault. Keep customer names and contact details off the worksheet.',
  },
  {
    term: 'What you’ll write',
    text: 'A parts enquiry ready to send to your own distributor.',
  },
];
---

<Workshop
  title="Write a parts enquiry from a repair order | Parts Relay"
  description="Use a repair order and the downloadable service desk worksheet to write an enquiry a supplier can price."
  heading="Write a parts enquiry from a repair order"
  audience="For repair shops. Self-guided, about 10 minutes."
  poster="/assets/workshop.webp"
  posterAlt="Service adviser at a workshop bench"
  prepare={prepare}
>
  <Fragment slot="intro">
    The person pricing a part can’t see the car. Read the technician’s note as if you’d just picked
    up the phone at the parts counter. Which detail would you ask for first?
  </Fragment>
  <section class="workshop-materials">
    <h2>Start with the job card</h2>
    <p>
      Download the worksheet and keep the repair order beside it. Check the VIN format once you have
      the vehicle details.
    </p>
    <div>
      <a
        href="/worksheets/parts-relay-first-request-worksheet.md"
        download="Parts-Relay-first-request-worksheet.md"
      >
        Download worksheet ↓
      </a>
      <a href="/tools/vin-check/">Check a VIN</a>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="workshop-agenda">
    <h2>When a symptom stands in for the part name</h2>
    <p>
      A door that won’t stay shut tells the counter about the fault. A damaged right rear latch
      tells them which item to look up. If the repair also needs an actuator, find out whether it
      comes with the latch.
    </p>
    <h2>Write up the request</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>
        <h3>Find the technician’s diagnosis</h3>
        <p>
          Underline the component they identified. If you can only find a symptom, ask what they
          intend to replace before requesting a price.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <h3>Describe one orderable item</h3>
        <p>
          Record the vehicle from the job card. Name the part with its fitting position and
          quantity. For a door latch, that means which door, which side and how many.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <h3>Ask about anything supplied with it</h3>
        <p>
          Turn the unresolved detail into a question the distributor can answer on the quote. If the
          actuator is needed, ask whether it comes with the latch or carries its own reference.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ol>
    <h2>Read it as the distributor</h2>
    <dl class="workshop-answer">
      <div>
        <dt>Vehicle</dt>
        <dd>Use the verified identification for this job.</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Item and quantity</dt>
        <dd>
          Would someone reading the enquiry know which door the latch goes on and how many to price?
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Technician’s finding</dt>
        <dd>
          Keep the finding that explains the scope of the repair. Additional symptoms belong on the
          job card unless they affect the choice of part.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Question for the counter</dt>
        <dd>
          Name the component whose inclusion needs confirming. “Does the latch come with the
          actuator?” gives the distributor something concrete to check.
        </dd>
      </div>
    </dl>
    <h2>Check it with the technician</h2>
    <p>
      Read the finished request to the technician. Correct any difference between the part they
      intend to fit and the item the supplier is about to price.
    </p>
    <h2>Using the sheet for a window repair</h2>
    <p>
      For a window regulator, record the affected door and ask whether the quoted regulator includes
      the motor.
    </p>
  </section>
</Workshop>
